WEBVTT

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.590
ADITYA BADRAMRAJU: Hi, everyone.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.410
I hope you are
having great Build.

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.400
I'm Aditya.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.580
I'm the Product Manager
from Azure SQL.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.990
Today, I'm here to talk to you

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.900
about building
scalable applications

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.970
without having polyglot tax.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.340
Now let's talk what
exactly is polyglot tax.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.840
What do we mean by that?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.610
When I say polyglot tax, I mean
having the tax that you pay

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:29.700
for choosing multiple databases.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.630
Okay. How many of you are
building applications day in,

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.100
day out?

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.050
I'm sure everyone.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.440
So let's say you
build a prototype.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.910
How long do you take?

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:46.710
Based on whatever
that application is,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.600
it takes -- you
start at 9:00 AM.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.210
Before lunch, you
almost have it.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.660
You can say, "Aditya,
it can be fast."

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.060
I'm sure it's for sure fast.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:00.660
So, but my point is you can
build a prototype pretty fast.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.790
But let's say you
built a prototype

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.280
and that prototype
is super successful.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.980
How do you -- and then
you want to add something

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.620
like semantic search
with vectors,

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.510
you might get a vector DB.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.960
And then you extended
it to have graphs.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.820
You added graph DB.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.520
And then you need to do
analytics on top of it.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:24.360
See what I'm doing here?

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.850
You add so many databases
that are specific to them.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.810
And now, because you are
adding so many databases,

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.710
in all of this data, in all
shipping all of this data,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.780
if something broke at one point,
the other things won't work.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:49.290
Imagine the data that flows
from vectors is broken,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.420
which comes to the analytics.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.840
The analytics might not
give you the right answers.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.310
So that is broken.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:01.440
So that's the polyglot
tax I'm talking about.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:08.220
We are here because this
one we can really avoid

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.080
with Microsoft SQL.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.390
Now, how do -- let's say
you're building a fraud check,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.360
real-time fraud
check application.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.450
How do you typically do that
with all these databases?

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.590
You do -- you have a relational
database for order history.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.770
You have a JSON or a
document databases

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:30.480
for device fingerprint.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.280
When I say device
fingerprint, is it coming

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.580
from an application, is it
coming from a browser, etc?

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.010
You might use a document
database for that.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.730
For detecting multiple
graph relations

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:45.840
between multiple people,
you might use graph.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:48.810
If you want to find
similar transactions like,

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.090
what would this
database look like?

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.030
What is this transaction
looking like?

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:59.580
Buying something from Walmart,
what does that look like?

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:02.010
Is there a similar
transaction to that?

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.750
There is that too.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:07.950
And then finally, you would
want all of this coming

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:11.580
to a statistical baseline, which
essentially means analytics.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.650
We have five
different databases,

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.070
five different sync jobs,
five different auth systems.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.550
And because of all of this,
you have so much latency

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:22.740
because your application
needs to wait

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.590
to read from all of this.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.030
The costs are also the same.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.290
That's what I'm trying to say.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:30.420
Because you are trying to
read from all of this,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.030
you have network latency.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.760
You have different
security surfaces

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.990
with all these databases.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:45.450
You have security in relational
versus DW versus your --

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.490
what do you say,
graph, all of that.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.880
You have separate auth
systems everywhere.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.710
You need to pay tax for that.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.170
You would have different
database backups

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.310
for all of this.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.660
Imagine that.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.220
There is no one single
backup statement

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.750
that would be doing this.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.230
Finally, more than anything,
there is cognitive load

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.020
so which means I need
to, as a developer,

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.840
I need to learn five
different tools,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.050
five different query
languages, etc.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:16.650
Now, as a user, it's
okay, but as an agent,

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:20.370
it's going to be even
more tough because agents

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.090
with so many choices tend
to hallucinate a lot.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.820
More hallucination
means more wrong data,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.150
which we really
doesn't want to have.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:34.830
That's why we are here
talking avoiding polyglot tax

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.870
with Microsoft SQL core engine.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:43.320
And not just with engine
avoiding the tax, you need scale

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:47.190
to perform and use the
best of this engine.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.490
That best of this engine comes
from Azure SQL Hyperscale.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.270
We will talk how Azure SQL
Hyperscale helps with that.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.970
So Act 1: how to avoid the tax

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.370
with all the different
capabilities that we built

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:02.460
into Microsoft SQL core engine.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.590
Act 2 is to how do you scale
that Microsoft SQL core engine,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:10.790
which is the base of building
the Azure SQL Hyperscale.

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:13.280
Okay. Let's go into Part 1.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.010
Okay. So let's talk
about the first act,

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:19.700
which is the core engine and how
we are avoiding the polyglot tax

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:20.900
with Microsoft SQL.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:28.650
Okay. So let's say
you have the --

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:34.800
as I mentioned in the fraud
detection app setting,

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.370
where you would need, if
you are logging from app

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.770
or if you are logging
from a web browser,

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:42.540
you need a JSON
kind of a document.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.990
And how would SQL
really do that?

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:52.470
So recently, SQL essentially
introduced JSON native type

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:59.160
and JSON indexes, and it is --
previously, when we started,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.120
we kind of did NVARCHAR MAX,
but it's no more NVARCHAR MAX.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.280
It's purely JSON type,
native JSON type, ANSI.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.250
And it is a pre-parsed binary,

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.160
which means the way
we store the JSON

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.610
in our engine is a
pre-parsed binary,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:22.410
which essentially makes
JSON save 30 to 50% less.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.890
And there is this
famous JSON index,

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:31.140
which in another relational
databases where you index.

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.190
When you index, the entire
JSON document gets indexed.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:37.320
The cool thing that
we did with JSON

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.640
in Microsoft SQL is you can
index the particular path

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:42.630
of that JSON.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.770
So that essentially makes
you feel that, okay,

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:51.870
if I want to search only on the
device, then whatever the date

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:56.340
or anything, I can index only on
the path of device in that JSON

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.660
and attributes, and your
JSON index will essentially

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:01.680
pick that.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:07.890
Now, with JSON, one of the
favorite features of using JSON,

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.590
even for the people in
document databases,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.650
is the schema flexibility.

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.740
You can have a JSON document
of, let's say, four attributes,

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:20.340
one of them, and another JSON
document of five attributes.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.050
In a relational
world, you might need

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:25.560
to add one more column
and all of that.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.390
That is not there with JSON.

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.280
And, hey, SQL supports it.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.780
Microsoft SQL supports it.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.840
And now, all the JSON
updates that you do

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:37.770
and what all you do follow the
same transaction boundary,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.900
and no more or nothing
more than that,

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.540
so which means you can
rollback, roll forward,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.540
all the ACID
properties are done.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:48.840
So we kind of see
the Microsoft SQL.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:55.620
We did -- internally,
we did the YCSW tests,

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:00.420
and we see that we are no less
than any document database

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.130
when it comes to
JSON performance

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.870
and JSON index performance.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.620
That's why we -- once you
do this, I almost kind

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.840
of say you need not really think

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.730
about having a separate
document database.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.740
You can essentially say goodbye,

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.130
and we just bank
on Microsoft SQL.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:23.190
Now, since forever, as I said,
we have invested in graph,

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.190
and you have graph
in Microsoft SQL.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:31.260
You can do MATCH, and you can
say the syntax is natively

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.150
supported, and you can have the
query optimizer understand this

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.120
MATCH and give you
a perfect plan

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.830
that is required for this MATCH.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:47.190
And that way, graph is inbuilt
in Microsoft SQL itself.

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.810
So you have JSON.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.010
You have graph.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:55.470
And there are graphs also
very interesting scenarios

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.700
when it comes to application
like anti-fraud detection,

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:01.380
fraud detection,
and all of that.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:07.260
So you really need -- wherever
you need a specialist graph kind

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.660
of scenarios, you need --

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:14.430
Microsoft SQL does support all
the basic graph functionalities,

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:18.840
such that you can use
Microsoft SQL for that.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:25.800
Now, recently, we started
supporting vectors.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.850
And when we started, again, we
used to support vectors on top

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:32.820
of varbinary, and then
we immediately switched

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.950
and vectors in native type.

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.430
Now, we initially
released vector type,

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.610
but we haven't released
the index on it.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:46.230
Later, we released vector
indexes using the DiskANN,

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.300
which is a great
Microsoft research,

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:50.760
which came out from this
Microsoft research,

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:56.430
and which is essentially
pretty fast and HNSW.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:10:01.320
And now, imagine vector
databases are kind of tricky.

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.710
If you have a specialized
vector database,

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:10.680
and if you don't have the
operational data's context

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:17.040
to it, what happens is you would
end up searching a lot number

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.860
of rows, a lot
number of vectors,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.230
and that is costly in general.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:27.630
Let's say you want to
search about person Bob.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:34.470
And that person Bob is in
one of the relational data.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:38.520
You find that person, and you
will select transactions similar

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.200
to that, which essentially gives
you more context, and hence,

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.380
it will help you -- it will
help you reduce the --

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.830
reduce the tax on it, and
it will be pretty fast.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:54.780
Hence, essentially,
it's a pre-filter.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:11:00.870
And we also kind of introduced
very recently updatable

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.400
DiskANN indexes.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.190
So this essentially makes people
who are vector databases kind

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:12.330
of -- there is no need of a
separate vector database.

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.850
Now, clustered columnstore.

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:21.300
This is one of the very famous
features that SQL have.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:22.920
And we are
continuously investing

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:32.850
in this columnstore today, where
-- so in SQL, traditionally used

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:35.880
to be its rowstore, you can
convert them to columnstore,

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:38.850
and which is more OLAP-friendly.

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:43.290
And all the data that you
have for the analytics,

00:11:43.290 --> 00:11:46.680
you can transfer them as a
columnstore, which essentially,

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:50.940
again, gives you a very huge
amount of compression for you

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.780
to -- on top of the
rowstore, and you can expose

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:57.540
that for your
analytics endpoint.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:00.750
So that essentially
gives you HTAP.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:06.120
One, you have the
primary transactions.

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:07.050
You have analytics.

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:08.520
You have JSON.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.580
But just having
transactions, analytics,

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:15.150
and the real-time transactions,
it makes Microsoft SQL one

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.550
of the best analytical
stores out there.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.580
So now, combine all this story.

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:22.560
Let's look at all of this.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:24.480
You have JSON.

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:30.300
You got the order history of
a person using relational.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.970
That's what I'm kind
of doing with this.

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:37.050
And then you kind of got the
device footprint from JSON,

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:40.110
saying -- by just
using JSON value.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:45.240
And then you said, "Okay,
for this, can you give me

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:50.850
for the MATCH of this -- for a
risk score greater than 0.8?

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.570
And then find the similar
transactions of that

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:59.220
in the vector table and give a
90-day baseline on top of it."

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:04.500
And I can write all of it in
a single stored procedure

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:09.270
and execute that, and it will
have one transaction boundary.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:13.830
Which means, let's say when you
are writing something on JSON,

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:16.260
if it fails, everything fails.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:20.550
This is very, very important
for agents because agents,

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:27.390
though one of the fact that we
learned recently is agent needs

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:29.340
a lot of data to read.

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.980
But agent also
loves idempotency,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:34.770
which means they
come, they write.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:38.220
If they write, they
might rewrite because of

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:42.210
so many things, which means
they need a proper rollback.

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:47.130
ACID semantics are so
important in agentic world

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:50.880
than a normal world
because in a normal world,

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:53.910
a developer would be sitting
and making sure that, oh,

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:57.090
this transaction has this
transaction boundary.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:02.310
And hence, when it comes to
agents, they just come in,

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.590
put the transaction, and go out.

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:09.180
That we should really make
sure that ACID is the only way

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:11.610
where you can know the
data that is coming

00:14:11.610 --> 00:14:14.940
in is completely coming
in and get committed.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:18.720
So it becomes even more
important in the agentic world.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.860
Now, that's the part
with core engine,

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:25.200
where you avoided polyglot tax.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.610
Now, take this core engine,
take it up, and you plug

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:33.210
that into something
which scales.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.650
That is Hyperscale.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:35.700
That is the Hyperscale,

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:39.390
and I call this databases
peace of mind database.

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:41.580
The reason why I call
this databases a peace

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:47.100
of mind database is
when you start --

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:48.270
there is a famous customer.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:50.460
He told me this story.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:53.190
He mentioned, "Hey, Aditya.

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.920
After we switched to Hyperscale,

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:58.620
I can really sleep
peacefully in the night."

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:01.350
That made me come up with
this peace of mind database

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.540
with some other colleagues.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:08.010
The reason why I
said that whatever

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.270
that customer told me is

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:12.810
so important is the
customer need not wake

00:15:12.810 --> 00:15:17.790
up for a file group full, or
a file full, or a log full,

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:21.150
or anything because
Hyperscale just grows

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:22.950
as your application grows.

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:25.740
And it has its own
dedicated log service.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:28.530
It has its own page
servers and Azure storage.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:34.410
So why I typically show
a architecture diagram,

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:37.800
here I'm not showing it
purposefully because all you,

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.930
as an audience, need

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:45.240
to understand here is Hyperscale
has different components.

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:49.380
You have log service, dedicated
log service for your writes.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.680
Your reads are coming
from page servers,

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:57.630
which are essentially a --
which have SSDs of its own,

00:15:57.630 --> 00:15:58.890
and it is asynchronous.

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:03.450
Log service will do continuous
redo into page service.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.780
This page service will
continuously write back

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:11.670
into Azure storage for the
durable -- as a durable tier.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:15.630
Now, if you do that, because you
are writing into Azure storage,

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:19.620
what happens is we can use
the Azure storage's tech

00:16:19.620 --> 00:16:21.510
of taking snapshots.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:25.860
Now, eventually, it became a
good news for all the users

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:29.250
because compute on the
top is not involved

00:16:29.250 --> 00:16:32.850
in taking the backups, which
means there is no pages

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.130
that were read to take a backup.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:38.550
Or in traditional SQL, if you
are doing backup database,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:41.310
database name, you are
essentially reading that backup,

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:42.960
reading all the data
into the compute

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.420
and writing it
back into the.bak.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:50.670
That is not needed anymore with
Hyperscale snapshot backups.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:58.360
Now, how, in general, with
agents and all of this world,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:02.730
people use Hyperscale.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:09.570
So the way they use is they use
primary for the OLTP writes.

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:11.100
One of the major things

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:15.570
that Hyperscale has a advantage
is having named replicas.

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:17.310
What this named replicas is,

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:20.160
think of it like you have
its own connection string

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:21.980
for a readable copy.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.570
Now, why is that important?

00:17:23.570 --> 00:17:27.920
That is important because that
readable copy essentially has

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.890
its own buffer pool, which
means its own memory,

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:32.850
its own L2 cache.

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:36.660
We call it as RBPEX, which is
resilient buffer pool extension,

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.150
which means it has
its own working set.

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:42.240
And that working set
is extremely different

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:44.820
to any other named
replica that you give.

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:48.870
So you can differentiate
working sets in Hyperscale

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:51.390
with different named replicas

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:56.130
with an underlying all
the same data of 128 TB.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:59.970
And you can have your writes
not affected by reads.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:04.550
So we see customers doing
writes on the primary.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.380
They are continuous writes.

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:12.470
We see customers doing
agents pointed to one

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:15.560
of the named replicas, and
they do vector distances,

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:17.270
similar transactions,
all of that,

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:20.750
and find the cosine similarity

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:23.150
and give back what
exactly it is.

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:26.160
So that from one named replica.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:30.740
I have seen customers
using nightly jobs,

00:18:30.740 --> 00:18:33.290
and they use serverless
specifically

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:36.420
for those named replicas,
where they go --

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:40.230
in the morning, the serverless
comes to two vCores,

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:44.940
and in the night when they use,
they expand it to 128 vCores.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:49.890
They run their job, run the
analytics, and they sleep off.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.710
So they use separate
named replica for that.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:58.080
By the way, your primary
can always be provisioned,

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:03.750
and your named replicas
can be serverless.

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.680
So we also care about your
business continuity; hence,

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:13.320
we do have Geo-DR and high
availability built-in

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:14.850
into Hyperscale.

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:19.890
Now, let's look at
the other side of it.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:23.160
Hyperscale comes -- because
Hyperscale also is a Microsoft

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:29.130
SQL core engine, the engine
itself has the best security

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:30.720
out there.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:31.860
Do you know something

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:38.220
that Microsoft SQL
supports ledger too?

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:41.460
That's what we support
even in Hyperscale

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:43.680
because Microsoft
SQL supports it.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:45.210
We have dynamic data masking.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:49.080
You can have always encrypted,

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:50.550
(inaudible) only
stored procedures,

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.470
tenant-based execution,

00:19:52.470 --> 00:19:56.250
all of that using
Microsoft SQL core engine

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:57.660
and also in Hyperscale.

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:04.920
Now, what is AI or
agent or any talk

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:07.380
without having an MCP server?

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:11.310
So SQL has its own MCP server.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.830
We did that with the
Data API builder.

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:18.540
You have so many
tools that you use --

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.270
you can use with
this MCP server.

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:24.450
You point the -- you have
a DAB, create a DAB.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:27.660
You created a Data API builder
using Data API builder.

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:32.460
You created that, and you get
your Data API REST endpoint.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:36.450
Using that REST endpoint, you
can point that to an MCP,

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:42.360
and that MCP can come from
Hyperscale, and then that's it.

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:46.740
So you can talk to your data,
which is in Hyperscale.

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:48.090
Imagine this.

00:20:48.090 --> 00:20:50.250
You have 128 TB data.

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:51.780
You have agents.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:53.580
You have writes.

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.260
And you have MCP.

00:20:55.260 --> 00:21:00.630
You can talk to all of this with
MCP with the natural language,

00:21:00.630 --> 00:21:04.650
saying, "Update
so-and-so person's name,

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:09.060
last name to this," or, "Change
the salary of so-and-so person

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:12.720
to this," all of it using MCP.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.030
That's what we have
with SQL MCP server.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:18.870
Yeah. So what is -- we
understand that core engine --

00:21:18.870 --> 00:21:22.920
now come back to core
engine is now --

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:28.080
doesn't have polyglot tax, and
Hyperscale is giving scale.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:31.950
Whatever it is, the economics
are very important.

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:36.720
So in the economics, because
you are avoiding polyglot tax,

00:21:36.720 --> 00:21:38.580
essentially, you are
eliminating all the sidecars,

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:41.430
which means separate vector
DB, separate graph DB.

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:45.780
All of them are not there, and
hence, you eliminate that tax.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:50.970
You only pay for what you use
with serverless, and it can go

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:54.210
from two vCores to 80 vCores,
but only when you need.

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.520
If not, you will come
back to the two vCores

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:58.920
and pay the less bill for that.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:03.450
You pay only for storage
from 10 GB to 128 TB.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:07.500
We won't over-provision like you
need not over-provision upfront,

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.810
and you don't need to pay bill.

00:22:09.810 --> 00:22:12.510
Whenever your data grows,
we grow with that,

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.330
which means even the
people cost is less

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:17.100
because you really
need not think

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:20.160
of having five different
personas doing five different

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:21.930
things and five
different expertise.

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:25.650
You can have one SQL expertise,
and that's pretty much it.

00:22:25.650 --> 00:22:30.480
So Hyperscale also -- imagine
all of this we are giving you

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:34.230
with a 68% better performance
than our compete.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:36.510
So that's a steal.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:39.960
So that's why we say
that Microsoft SQL,

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.370
combined with Azure
SQL Hyperscale,

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:42.900
has the best economics.

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:45.270
By the way, one
point I really want

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:53.430
to touch upon is Azure SQL
Hyperscale is the database

00:22:53.430 --> 00:22:57.270
with licensing-free model.

00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:02.280
Now, so ultimately,
what is here.

00:23:02.280 --> 00:23:06.630
So two acts, five ideas
I will leave you with.

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:10.500
Polyglot tax is a choice,
not an inevitability,

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.770
which means it's not
really needed to go

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:15.570
to polyglot tax choice.

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:17.940
But I also want to
ground upon a truth

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:20.430
that one size doesn't fit all.

00:23:20.430 --> 00:23:23.970
So there are cases where,
in the extreme cases,

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:29.310
you might need a DQP, and you
really need a data warehouse.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:31.650
In the extreme cases of graph,

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:33.930
you might need a separate
graph databases.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:38.220
But all the basic scenario,
all the scenarios where,

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:42.060
for the most of the scenarios,
unless it is exceptional,

00:23:42.060 --> 00:23:45.690
Microsoft SQL covers you with an
exceptional relational workload.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:47.460
So that's what we are saying.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:49.410
And Hyperscale
architecture is one

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:54.330
of the best architectures
purpose-built for cloud.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:59.340
And agents, as I mentioned
in the talk, really,

00:23:59.340 --> 00:24:03.780
really need all these
guarantees more than ever.

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:09.000
So Microsoft SQL and Azure
SQL Hyperscale as a story is

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.450
so shining right now than ever.

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:16.770
So all I say is avoid
the tax, ship at scale,

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:19.260
and yeah, sleep at night.

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:20.650
Thank you.
